Check the unix socket peer on the BSDs, and size the address with offsetof.

IsPeerValid() reads SO_PEERCRED and struct ucred, which is Linux.  Each BSD
spells the peer credential differently: LOCAL_PEEREID with unpcbid on NetBSD,
SO_PEERCRED with sockpeercred on OpenBSD, LOCAL_PEERCRED with xucred on
FreeBSD and DragonFly.  DragonFly's xucred carries no pid, so *pid stays 0
there and IsValidServer() takes its existing pid == 0 path.

The address length is a separate bug the BSDs make visible: sizeof(sun_family)
is not the offset of sun_path, and a BSD sockaddr_un leads with sun_len, so
bind() and connect() were given a length one short and the kernel dropped the
last character of the path.  offsetof is right everywhere, Linux included.

This is the 3.33.6089 shape of the second commit on zakinko/mozc bsd-support;
the file moved between 3.33 and 3.34.  CI only.

--- a/ipc/unix_ipc.cc	2026-09-23 14:17:06
+++ b/ipc/unix_ipc.cc	2026-09-23 14:17:37
@@ -38,6 +38,11 @@
 #include <sys/un.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
 
+#if defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__DragonFly__)
+// struct xucred lives here, and sys/un.h does not pull it in.
+#include <sys/ucred.h>
+#endif  // __FreeBSD__ || __DragonFly__
+
 #include <cerrno>
 #include <cstddef>
